; 29-numbers-sets-and-sorts.kurt
;
; Numbers and sets together: sorts
;
; Kurt checks that a number is not used as a set where that makes no sense: `{1, 2} + 0` is a type
; error. It does that with *sorts*, five rules in all:
;
;   1. The sorts form a hierarchy with `any` on top: `real ⊂ set ⊂ any` (set.kurt). A number is
;      a set (as in set theory), every object is an `any`.
;   2. Every symbol has a sort; without one it is `any`, any object. A constant gets its sort at its
;      *first sorted use*, and that is final.
;   3. A value fits a place of its own sort or a bigger one: a number fits where a set is required.
;   4. `∀ x` and `∃ x` range over the sort of `x`. A fact gives no sort.
;   5. A rule for every sort writes its bound variables `$$x` (for theory authors, see the end).
;
; Sorts are lowercase, sets capitalized: `real x` says what kind of thing `x` is, `x ∈ Real` is a
; fact about it (`Real`, also `ℝ`, is the set of all numbers; `Nat`, `Int`, `Rat` are sets too).
;
; Run it: `kurt 29-numbers-sets-and-sorts.kurt`. The lines after `expect` are errors on purpose.
load natural

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 2. A constant gets its sort at its first sorted use

const x, A
x ∈ A ⇒ x ∈ A                         ; `x` has no sort yet: the left of `∈` takes any object
x + 0 = x                             ; the first sorted use: `x` is a number from here on (see `info x`)
info x
A ⊂ A ⇒ A ⊂ A                         ; `A` is a set

; ... and that is final: a later use at another sort is a type error, which names both places and
; says what to do. (A number is a set, so a *declared* number may be used as a set.)
const s
use s ⊂ A                             ; `s` is a set
expect "TypeError" "declare `real s` before that use"
    use s + 0 = s
real t                                 ; declared first: a number, which fits where a set is required
use t ⊂ A
use t + 0 = t

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 3. A value fits a place of its own sort or a bigger one

3 ∈ {3}                               ; a number where an element (any object) is required
expect "TypeError" "must have sort `real`"
    use {1, 2} + 0 = {1, 2}           ; a set where a number is required: junk, refused

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 4. Binders range over the sort of their variable -- and a fact gives no sort

; `∀ y` without a sort is over all objects, so it meets extensionality: two sets with the same
; elements are equal
const B, C
(∀ y (y ∈ B ⇔ y ∈ C)) ⇒ B = C         ; by "extensionality"

; a binder whose variable is a number by its places is over the numbers: `∀ n (n + 0 = n)`
∀ n (n + 0 = n)                       ; ("add-identity", over the numbers)

; a fact gives no sort: `b ∈ Nat` says that `b` is a natural number, but not that `b` is of sort `real`.
; Declare it, or let its first sorted use be a number:
set b
use b ∈ Nat
expect "TypeError" "a fact `b ∈ Nat` gives no sort"
    use b + 1 = 1 + b
const c
use c ∈ Nat
c + 1 = 1 + c                         ; `c`'s first sorted use: a number

; A witness has the sort of the existential's variable: from `∃ y (y ∈ A)`, over all objects, the
; witness is any object -- not a number, whatever you know about it
use ∃ y (y ∈ A)
pick w with w ∈ A
    expect "TypeError" "must have sort `real`"
        use w + 0 = w
    true

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; `default real`: a file about numbers says so once
;
; With `default real` at the top (after `load natural`), every new symbol without a declared sort is
; a number from its first appearance -- the variables of a theorem about the natural numbers, and
; what induction needs (lesson 11). Without a default, a symbol is any object until its first
; sorted use (`default any` is the same as no default).

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 5. For theory authors: rules for every sort, `$$x`
;
; The rules of logic.kurt hold for every sort; their bound variables are written `$$x`:
;
;     use ∀ $$x %A  implies  sub $$x $a %A      "forall-elim"
;
; One use of such a rule is at one sort (all its `$$` binders over the same sort), and a `$$x` may
; occur only in its binder, in `sub` and as a side of `=` -- where the sort doesn't matter. Kurt
; checks both. Why it is sound: doc/kurt-soundness.md §10, doc/kurt-kernel.md §8.
